{{Politics of Macedonia}}

General elections{{ref label|A|a|a}} were held in the [[Republic of Macedonia]] in April 2014 to elect the [[President of the Republic of Macedonia|President]] and members of [[Assembly of the Republic of Macedonia|parliament]]. The first round of the presidential elections were held on 13 April, with incumbent president [[Gjorge Ivanov]] finishing first with 53% of the vote. However, as he did not receive the support of 50% of all registered voters, a second round was held on 27 April, alongside parliamentary elections, with Ivanov and the ruling coalition led by [[VMRO-DPMNE]] claiming victory as Ivanov was elected president and the VMRO-DPMNE won 61 of the 123 seats in the [[Assembly of North Macedonia|Assembly]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://au.news.yahoo.com/world/a/22620503/macedonia-presidential-election-set-for-second-vote/ | title=Macedonia presidential election set for second vote | publisher=[[Yahoo News]] Australia | work=[[Agence France-Presse]] | date=14 April 2014 | access-date=19 April 2014 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140420032042/https://au.news.yahoo.com/world/a/22620503/macedonia-presidential-election-set-for-second-vote/ | archive-date=20 April 2014 | df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-macedonia-elections-idUSBREA250XI20140306 | title=Macedonia calls early parliamentary election for April 27 | publisher=Reuters | date=6 March 2014 | access-date=19 April 2014 | author=Kole Casule}}</ref>

==Background==
The parliamentary elections were brought forward to coincide with the presidential election following the failure of coalition partners VMRO-DPMNE and [[Democratic Union for Integration|DUI]] to agree on a combined presidential candidate.<ref name="dw">{{cite web|url=http://www.dw.de/macedonia-goes-to-the-polls-in-early-parliamentary-vote/a-17594202 |title=Macedonia wraps up voting in early parliamentary vote &#124; News &#124; DW.DE &#124; 27.04.2014 |publisher=DW.DE |date=2014-04-13 |access-date=2014-04-30}}</ref>

==President==
===Candidates===
The incumbent president Gjorge Ivanov, supported by the governing party, the Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization – Democratic Party for Macedonian National Unity ([[VMRO-DPMNE]]), successfully nominated himself for reelection after collecting 63,253 signatures from citizens.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://kurir.mk/makedonija/vesti/149896-Masovna-poddrska-za-Ivanov-Sobrani-63253-potpisi-za-nekolku-casa | title=Масовна поддршка за Иванов: Собрани 63.253 потписи за неколку часа | publisher=Kurir.mk | date=4 March 2014 | access-date=19 April 2014 | language=mk | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140419210753/http://kurir.mk/makedonija/vesti/149896-Masovna-poddrska-za-Ivanov-Sobrani-63253-potpisi-za-nekolku-casa | archive-date=19 April 2014 | df=dmy-all }}</ref> The [[Social Democratic Union of Macedonia]]  (SDSM) candidate [[Stevo Pendarovski]] was nominated by the signatures of the opposition parties' members of parliament.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://a1on.mk/wordpress/archives/294169 | title=СДСМ: Зад кандидатурата на Пендаровски стојат потписите на сите пратеници на СДСМ и коалиционите партнери | publisher=a1on.mk | date=6 March 2014 | access-date=19 April 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140420012144/http://a1on.mk/wordpress/archives/294169 | archive-date=20 April 2014 | url-status=dead }}</ref> Other candidates nominated by over 10,000 signatures included [[Zoran T. Popovski]] from the [[Citizen Option for Macedonia]] (GROM), and {{ill|Ilijaz Halimi|bg|Илияз Халими}} from the [[Democratic Party of Albanians]] (DPA).<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.sitel.com.mk/mk/ilijaz-halimi-e-kandidat-na-dpa-za-pretsedatel-na-makedonija-0 | title=Илијаз Халими е кандидат на ДПА за претседател на Македонија | publisher=[[Sitel (TV channel)|Sitel]] | date=26 February 2014 | access-date=6 April 2014 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140305123807/http://www.sitel.com.mk/mk/ilijaz-halimi-e-kandidat-na-dpa-za-pretsedatel-na-makedonija-0 | archive-date=5 March 2014 | df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.dnevnik.mk/default.asp?ItemID=AD7369A95668E34FB6794AF1F68AED22 | title=Зоран Поповски веќе собра 10.000 потписи | language=mk | publisher=[[Dnevnik (Skopje)|Dnevnik]] | date=17 February 2014 | access-date=6 April 2014 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140304162112/http://www.dnevnik.mk/default.asp?ItemID=AD7369A95668E34FB6794AF1F68AED22 | archive-date=4 March 2014 | df=dmy-all }}</ref>

===Campaign===
The first round of the presidential election was held on 13 April. The candidates were Gjorge Ivanov (VMRO-DPMNE), Stevo Pendarovski (SDSM), Ilijaz Halimi (DPA), and Zoran Popovski (GROM). The [[Albanians in North Macedonia|ethnic Albanian]] party [[Democratic Union for Integration]] (DUI), a junior coalition member, campaigned for boycott of the presidential election, opposing VMRO-DPMNE's decision to run Ivanov for reelection.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://news.yahoo.com/ethnic-albanians-boycott-macedonia-vote-184654988.html | title=Ethnic Albanians boycott Macedonia vote | publisher=[[Yahoo News]] | agency=Associated Press | date=13 April 2014 | access-date=19 April 2014 | author=Konstantin Testorides}}</ref>

SDSM's Pendarovski visited [[Pristina]] where he criticised the government policies in terms of [[Foreign relations of the Republic of Macedonia|foreign policy]] and that Albania has the highest GDP in the region despite reports by institutions and other politicians saying it was Macedonia.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://kurir.mk/en/2014/04/25/here-is-how-pendarovski-january-slanders-his-spine-in-prishtina-praises-kosovo-and-criticizes-macedonia/ |title=Here is how Pendarovski January slanders his spine: In Prishtina praises Kosovo and criticizes Macedonia &#124; Macedonian News Agency |publisher=Kurir.mk |date=2014-04-25 |access-date=2014-04-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140427223953/http://kurir.mk/en/2014/04/25/here-is-how-pendarovski-january-slanders-his-spine-in-prishtina-praises-kosovo-and-criticizes-macedonia/ |archive-date=27 April 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

==Parliament==
===Parties and coalitions===
Fourteen political parties and coalitions contested the election, having submitted candidate lists for MPs in at least one of the six constituencies within the country and the three in the diaspora. Three among them, namely, VMRO-DPMNE, SDSM and DUI, submitted their candidate lists in all nine constituencies.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.utrinski.mk/?ItemID=288A1834285C3A438EAA163D805B090F |title=Четиринаесет партии доставија листи на кандидати за пратеници |publisher=Utrinski.mk |date=2014-03-30 |access-date=2014-04-30 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140428023118/http://www.utrinski.mk/?ItemID=288A1834285C3A438EAA163D805B090F |archive-date=28 April 2014 |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

====VMRO-DPMNE====
The VMRO-DPMNE-led coalition consisted of 22 parties: VMRO-DPMNE, the [[Socialist Party of Macedonia]] (SPM), the [[Democratic Union (North Macedonia)|Democratic Union]] (DS), [[Democratic Renewal of Macedonia]] (DOM), the [[Democratic Party of Turks]] (DPTM), the [[Democratic Party of Serbs in Macedonia]] (DPSM), the [[Union of Roma in Macedonia]] (SRM), the [[United Party for Emancipation]] (OPE), the [[Party of Justice]] (PP), the Party of the {{ill|Democratic Action of Macedonia|sv|Demokratisk aktion i Makedonien}} (SDAM), the [[Party of the Vlachs of Macedonia]] (PVM), the {{ill|Party for Integration of the Roma|mk|Партија за интеграција на Ромите|sv|Partiet för romsk integration}} (PIR), the {{ill|Bosniak Democratic Party|sv|Bosniakernas demokratiska parti}} (BDP), {{ill|Democratic Forces of the Roma|mk|Демократски сили на Ромите|sv|Romernas demokratiska krafter i Makedonien}} (DSR), {{ill|Permanent Macedonian Radical Unification|mk|Трајно македонско радикално обединување}} (TMRO), the {{ill|New Liberal Party (North Macedonia)|mk|Нова либерална партија|lt=New Liberal Party}} (NLP), the {{ill|People's Movement for Macedonia|mk|Единствена Македонија}} (NDM), {{ill|VMRO–Democratic Party|mk|Внатрешна македонска револуционерна организација - Демократска партија}}, VMRO-United, {{ill|Fatherland's Macedonian Organisation for Radical Renewal–Vardar–Aegean–Pirin|mk|Татковинска македонска организација за радикална обнова - Вардар - Егеј - Пирин}} ({{nobr|TMORO–VEP}}), {{ill|Macedonian Alliance|mk|Македонска алијанса}} (MA), and {{nobr|VMRO–Macedonian}}.<ref name=KAS/>

====SDSM====
The SDSM-led coalition included nine parties; the [[Social Democratic Union of Macedonia]], the [[New Social Democratic Party]] (NDSP), the [[Liberal Democratic Party (North Macedonia)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), [[United for Macedonia]] (OM), the {{ill|Party for the Movement of Turks in Macedonia|mk|Партија за движење на Турците во Македонија|sv|Rörelsen för turkar i Makedonien}} (PDT), the [[Party for the Full Emancipation of the Roma of Macedonia]] (PCER), the {{ill|Serbian Party in Macedonia|mk|Српска странка во Македонија}} (SSM), the [[Democratic Union of the Vlachs of Macedonia]] (DSVM), and the {{ill|Sandžak List|mk|Санџачка лига}} (SL).<ref name=KAS/>

====GROM====
The [[Citizen Option for Macedonia]] (GROM)-led alliance consisted of the [[Citizen Option for Macedonia]], the [[Liberal Party of Macedonia|Liberal Party]], the [[Serbian Progressive Party in Macedonia]], the [[Union of Tito's Left Forces]], and the Party of Free 
Democrats.<ref name=KAS>[http://www.kas.de/wf/doc/kas_37896-1522-1-30.pdf?140529150937  The Republic of Macedonia's 2014 Parliamentary Elections Handbook] Konrad Adenauer Stiftung</ref>

===Campaign===
SDSM's [[Zoran Zaev]] said that the election was about "choosing whether [the country] will support the fight for freedom and the right to a better life, or continue with state robbery."<ref name="aljaz">{{cite web|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/europe/2014/04/macedonia-pm-tipped-win-third-term-201442745347375961.html |title=PM tipped for third term as Macedonia votes - Europe |publisher=Al Jazeera English |access-date=2014-04-30}}</ref> Prime Minister Nikola Gruevski said: "We need a majority so nobody can blackmail us and we can keep up with the programme...that would lead Macedonia into the EU and NATO."<ref name="dw"/>

