==Conduct==
The second round of the election had accreditations given to 9,952 domestic and 550 foreign observers, as well as 283 translators, according to the State Election Commission.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://kurir.mk/en/2014/04/25/9952-domestic-and-550-foreign-observers-will-follow-elections/ |title=9952 domestic and 550 foreign observers will follow elections &#124; Macedonian News Agency |publisher=Kurir.mk |date=2014-04-25 |access-date=2014-04-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140427222645/http://kurir.mk/en/2014/04/25/9952-domestic-and-550-foreign-observers-will-follow-elections/ |archive-date=27 April 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

The United States and [[European Union]] publicly urged political leaders to ensure the election was "credible and transparent," amidst complains by the SDSM.<ref name="aljaz"/>

==Results==
===President===
In second round voting, centres were open from 5:00 GMT to 17:00 GMT. Turnout was reported as 9.58% in the first three hours.<ref name="aljaz"/> Gjorge Ivanov was re-elected with 57% of the valid vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://24vesti.mk/rezultati-spored-dik-za-pretsedatelskite-izbori-za-9823-od-glasovite |title=ДИК: Иванов 534.154 гласови, Пендаровски 398.008 &#124; 24 ВЕСТИ |publisher=24vesti.mk |date=2014-04-15 |access-date=2014-04-30}}</ref> Following the election, Stevo Pendarovski called for an investigation of the election by external observers.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.utrinski.mk/?ItemID=84E14D42189E7F4BB7BD0EAE7157AF78 |title=4471 |publisher=Utrinski.mk |access-date=2014-04-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140430024845/http://www.utrinski.mk/?ItemID=84E14D42189E7F4BB7BD0EAE7157AF78 |archive-date=30 April 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

